  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 252 AM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nThe northern stream will begin to influence our weather by Thursday. \nThis should help steer the upper low from Colorado into \nwestern/central Kansas by Thursday afternoon. Moisture from the \nremnants of Hurricane Polo will have been absorbed into the the \nupper low's circulation by Thursday. It appears that a drier, \nCanadian airmass associated with high pressure, will be building \nsouthward through the day Thursday. As the precipitation/rain \nassociated with the upper low Thursday tries to wrap north and \nnorthwest, it will encounter this drier air, so not expecting much \nto make it into our area. The best chances will be across far \nsouthwest Nebraska near the Kansas border. By Thursday evening, the \nentire system quickly fills and weakens as it moves into eastern \nKansas. Rain chances will completely end for all areas by Thursday \nevening. Dry conditions are expected to then persist through the \nupcoming weekend. Dry, northwest flow aloft, becomes dominate to the \nnortheast of expanding upper-level high pressure across the desert \nsouthwest.\n\nMuch below normal temperatures are expected Thursday into Friday as \nthe Canadian airmass settles southward into the plains. Lows \nThursday night will be the coolest so far this season, with lower to \nmid 40s forecast. Temperatures do begin to moderate some heading \ninto the weekend and early next week, but still expecting \ntemperatures to remain somewhat below average. Glancing over the NBM \n10th percentile lows through this period indicate mid 30s or \nwarmer. So not expecting a frost, or especially a freeze during this \ntime frame.",
